# Implementation, recovery, and verification

<!-- LH-RUN-01 -->
Read AGENTS.md, applicable implementation rules, the existing Spec/Plan, and current authorization; confirm the actual repository and existing changes. Choose Wiki, source, or both according to the goal. A precisely located repair can start in code; cross-module questions can use the hierarchy and sections. Do not impose a query sequence or infer actions from isolated words such as check or run.

<!-- LH-RUN-02 -->
Identify the requested outcome:
- **Implement or repair:** change only the authorized scope; follow the Exec Plan and maintain current progress, decisions, and the next action.
- **Verify or diagnose only:** run applicable checks or reproduce the issue; report outcomes, confidence in causes, and coverage limits. Failure does not authorize repair. Evidence writes must also fit the request; when no file changes are allowed, return a report only.

<!-- LH-RUN-03 -->
Before starting or resuming implementation, use [Stage readiness and handoff](../lumine-plan/references/stage-handoff.md) to verify the applicable Spec, design, Exec Plan, and confirmation to enter Run. Report missing confirmation; do not write a Plan and begin work on the strength of earlier broad authorization. Use lumine-plan when complex features lack an executable approach; stage transitions still require confirmation. A qualifying local maintenance or repair request may omit persistent Spec/Plan documents, but needs an explicit request, goal, boundaries, and verification; this exception cannot bypass product-change confirmations. Implement autonomously within the stage. Record local technical adjustments in the Plan; return material product or design changes to the relevant deliverable for confirmation of the affected scope, preserving unrelated valid confirmations.

<!-- LH-RUN-04 -->
Verification is part of Run and may happen during or after implementation without another stage confirmation. Still respect authorization boundaries for credentials, destructive actions, deployment, and external operations. Verify behavior as well as structure. Record actions, outcomes, time, code/environment scope, and evidence for applicable acceptance items. Distinguish tests, real operation, deployment, and user acceptance. Runtime checks do not replace behavioral judgment, and a pass statement is not evidence. Put detailed records in docs/validation/; keep summaries and links in the Plan.

<!-- LH-RUN-05 -->
Verify important gaps, stale explanations, and contradictions, then update affected knowledge within authorization. Preserve stable implementation changes and relevant upstream/downstream implications; add new modules to the hierarchy. Consult lumine-knowledge as needed, leave unrelated pages alone, and allow zero updates when no reusable knowledge was gained. Explicit read-only requests do not permit file changes. In-scope knowledge work belongs to completion; unrelated debt does not block it. A completed diagnosis may report failures without repairs. Use the three WORK_STATUS values done/continue/blocked, explaining blockers and next actions separately.

<!-- LH-RUN-06 -->
Parallel writers need exclusive file ownership, inputs, acceptance, and stopping conditions. Integrate serially and verify runtime behavior after relevant changes are integrated. Preserve repository, read-only business, and publication boundaries; implementation authorization does not imply permission to commit, push, deploy, or perform other external actions.
